"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 19. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4, pathological stage pT3a with extraprostatic extension, and positive surgical margins. Conversely, the seminal vesicles were not invaded, and lymphovascular invasion was absent. The absence of documented nodal sampling introduces uncertainty, as the status is pNx, which does not equate to pN0. Biopsy results showed a progression from Gleason 3+3 to 3+4 over time. The difference between biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this assessment. The current heuristic horizon suggests a follow-up period within a range of 44 to 91 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 67.48 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 43.86–91.10 months; its coverage has not been validated."
